-- title:
--   Pinned idempotent pieces of B mod p identified with A
-- statement:
--   The setting is fixed by the following data. A prime $p$ and a non-zero level $M$ with $p \mid M$ (`hpM`) and $p^2 \nmid M$ (`hpM2`), a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb Z/M)^\times$ containing every unit that maps to $1$ in $(\mathbb Z/(M/p))^\times$ (`hHp`), with $M/p$ non-zero; a valuation subring $Pl$ of an algebraic closure of $\mathbb Q$ whose set of non-units contains $p$ (`hPl`) and whose residue field is algebraically closed of characteristic $p$; the hypothesis `hj` that the $q$-expansion `jqModC ℚ` of $j$ lies in the level-one $q$-expansion function field; a Deligne–Rapoport model datum $\mathfrak X$ for $X_H$ at $p$; level data $\Lambda$ (a structure map $\sigma_A$, a scheme $\Lambda.X$ over the base `base p` with relative group law $\Lambda.L$ and parametrisations of points at the generic and special fibres) and a Néron object $O$ for $J_H$ at $p$ over $\Lambda$; the hypotheses `hrep`, `hrepΛ`, each asserting that the designation built from $(O.G, O.g)$, respectively $(\Lambda.X, \Lambda.f)$, together with its unit section, represents the subfunctor of rigidified line bundles that are fibrewise algebraically trivial, on the two-chart integral model `toBase p (ΓM M H) hj` rigidified by $\mathfrak X.\varepsilon_{\inf}$, respectively on `toBase p (ΓN p M H hpM) hj` rigidified by $\mathfrak X.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ followed by $\mathfrak X.\pi$.
--
--   Next, a ring $Rh$ which is a henselian local domain and a discrete valuation ring, an algebra over the algebraic closure of $\mathbb Q$ with faithful scalar action, such that every element of $Rh$ has image in $Pl$ (`hRA`) and lies in the maximal ideal exactly when that image has valuation $< 1$ (`hRloc`); an algebra structure of $Rh$ on $\mathbb Z/p$ whose kernel is described by the same valuation condition (`hres`). Finally a set of primes $S$ and a unit $d \in (\mathbb Z/M)^\times$ whose image in $\mathbb Z/(M/p)$ is $p$ (`hd`).
--
--   The arithmetic input is a $p$-divisible group $\mathcal G$ over $Rh$ of height $h$, a homomorphism $\Delta$ from its points over the algebraic closure of $\mathbb Q$ to $J_H = \mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the function field of $X_H$ over that closure, and a $\mathbb Z_p$-linear map $e$ of Tate modules. The finite-part witness consists of: injectivity of $\Delta$ (`hΔinj`); the identification `hΔlev` of `O.finPts (p ^ v)` — the subgroup generated by the $p^v$-torsion classes whose associated point extends to the place — with the image under $\Delta$ of the level-$v$ points; Galois equivariance of $\Delta$ (`hΔgal`); realisation of each Hecke generator [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) by a family of bialgebra endomorphisms of the levels of $\mathcal G$, compatible with the transition maps and inducing `genOpH` on points (`hΔhecke`); the componentwise description of $e$ by $\Delta$ (`he`), its injectivity (`heinj`), the description of its range as the sequences whose $n$-th entry lies in `O.finPts (p ^ n)` (`herange`), its Galois equivariance with respect to `JH.tateGaloisRep` (`hegal`), saturation of its range (`hsat`), and a $\mathbb Z_p$-linear isomorphism of the cokernel with $\mathbb Z_p^{O.\mathrm{toricRank}}$ (`hcoker`); and `htor`, that every class in `O.toricPts (p ^ v)` comes from a level-$v$ point.
--
--   The Raynaud quotient is a $p$-divisible group $\mathcal B$ over $Rh$ of height $hB$ together with bialgebra maps $\psi_v : \mathcal B.\mathrm{level}\,v \to \mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v$, subject to: $h = O.\mathrm{toricRank} + hB$ (`hhB`), $hB = 2h'$ for some $h'$ (`hhB2`), compatibility with transitions (`hψt`), the identification `hψker` of the kernel of the induced map on level-$v$ points with the classes lying in `O.toricPts (p ^ v)`, surjectivity on points (`hψsurj`), the reduction criterion `hψred` (if the image point is congruent to the counit modulo the maximal ideal of $Pl$, so is the original point), and the period condition `hperiod` (for $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $Pl$ over $\mathbb Q$ and $z$ a $p^v$-torsion class, a level-$v$ point with $\Delta$-image $\sigma \cdot z - z$ has trivial reduction in $\mathcal B$).
--
--   The scheme-level pins are a ring map $\rho_h : R_p \to Rh$ compatible with the maps to the algebraic closure of $\mathbb Q$ (`hρh`) and morphisms $\iota_v : \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v \to O.G$ over the base (`hιbase`), which are closed immersions into the pullback (`hιcl`), land in the $p^v$-kernel (`hιp`), compute the points $O.\mathrm{pts}(\Delta(\cdot))$ (`hιpts`), are homomorphic for the group law (`hιmul`), are compatible with the transitions (`hιt`), intertwine the bialgebra Hecke endomorphisms with `O.hecke` (`hιhecke`), and, via `hιfin`, induce a morphism $j_v$ into the $p^v$-kernel over $\operatorname{Spec} Rh$ that is both an open and a closed immersion and whose image contains every point over the closed point of $Rh$.
--
--   The operators are pinned as follows: bialgebra endomorphisms $u_v$ of the levels of $\mathcal G$, compatible with transitions (`hut`) and intertwined by $\iota$ with `O.hecke S (CohCarrier.Gen.U p _ hpM)` (`huι`); bialgebra automorphisms $Dp_v$, compatible with transitions (`hDpt`) and intertwined with `O.hecke S (CohCarrier.Gen.dia d)` (`hDpι`); both preserve the property of having $\Delta$-image in `O.toricPts (p ^ v)` (`hutor`, `hDptor`). On $\mathcal B$ there are endomorphisms $uB_v$, automorphisms $DB_v$, and automorphisms $DB'_v$ of $\mathbb Z/p \otimes_{Rh} \mathcal B.\mathrm{level}\,v$, with $u \circ \psi = \psi \circ uB$ (`huB`), $Dp \circ \psi = \psi \circ DB$ (`hDB`), and $DB'$ equal to the base change of $DB$ along $Rh \to \mathbb Z/p$ (`hDB'`).
--
--   Over the special fibre: a point $\sigma_p : \operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z/p \to$ `base p` arising from the composite of $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $Rh \to \mathbb Z/p$ with $\operatorname{Spec}\rho_h$ (`hσp`); morphisms $\iota^p_v$ from $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb Z/p \otimes_{Rh} \mathcal G.\mathrm{level}\,v)$ to the fibre product of $O.g$ and $\sigma_p$ with prescribed components (`hιp₁`, `hιp₂`); and two morphisms $q_0, q_1$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z/p$ from the base change of $O.g$ along $\sigma_p$ to the base change of $\Lambda.f$, each a homomorphism for the base-changed group laws (`hqmul`) and compatible with `O.abqFibre` after base change to the residue field of $Pl$ (`hqbc`), where $\sigma_p$ and $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ agree over that residue field (`hfac`).
--
--   The idempotent data are morphisms $r_0, r_1$ from $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the levels of $\mathcal B$ base changed to $\mathbb Z/p$ into the fibre product of $\Lambda.f$ and $\sigma_p$, and bialgebra endomorphisms $\varepsilon_v, \varepsilon'_v$ of those levels, subject to the fourteen clauses of `hidem`: for each $i$ and $v$, $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the base change of $\psi_v$ followed by $r_i$ equals $\iota^p_v$ followed by $q_i$; $\operatorname{Spec}\varepsilon_v$ followed by $r_1$ is the unit section and followed by $r_0$ is $r_0$, while $\operatorname{Spec}\varepsilon'_v$ followed by $r_0$ is the unit section and followed by $r_1$ is $r_1$; $\varepsilon$ and $\varepsilon'$ are idempotent; each of the two composites $\varepsilon \circ \varepsilon'$ and $\varepsilon' \circ \varepsilon$ equals the composite of the counit with the structure map; their convolution product equals the identity; both are compatible with the transitions; $\varepsilon_v \circ \widetilde{uB}_v \circ \varepsilon_v = \varepsilon_v \circ \widetilde{uB}_v$ for the base change $\widetilde{uB}$ of $uB$; and $\varepsilon_v$ commutes with $DB'_v$.
--
--   The level-$(M/p)$ side is a $p$-divisible group $\mathcal A$ over `baseRing p` of height $h\Lambda$, with $\sigma_p$ equal to $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the structure map `baseRing p` $\to \mathbb Z/p$ (`hσp'`), and morphisms $\iota'_v$ from $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the levels of $\mathcal A$ base changed to $\mathbb Z/p$ into the fibre product of $\Lambda.f$ and $\sigma_p$, which lie over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb Z/p$ (`hι'base`), are closed immersions (`hι'cl`), induce an isomorphism onto the $p^v$-kernel of the base-changed group law $\Lambda.L$ (`hι'p`), are homomorphic for that law (`hι'mul`), and are compatible with the transitions (`hι't`).
--
--   The Eichler–Shimura data consist of endomorphisms $U, D$ of the base change of $O$ along $\sigma_p$ lifting `O.hecke S (CohCarrier.Gen.U p _ hpM)` and `O.hecke S (CohCarrier.Gen.dia d)` (`hU`, `hD`), endomorphisms $F$, $V$, $D_\Lambda$ of the base change of $\Lambda$ with $V$ followed by $F$ equal to multiplication by $p$ (`hVF`) and $D_\Lambda$ a homomorphism (`hDΛmul`), the three identities `hE2` ($D$ followed by $q_1$ equals $q_1$ followed by $D_\Lambda$; $D$ followed by $q_0$ equals $q_0$ followed by $D_\Lambda$; $U$ followed by $q_1$ equals $q_1$ followed by $F$ followed by $D_\Lambda$), and `hE1`: for any point $x$ of the base-changed $O$ over a base $s$ whose $q_1$-component is the unit, $x$ followed by $U$ followed by $q_0$ equals $x$ followed by $q_0$ followed by $V$. Finally there are bialgebra endomorphisms $\varphi_V, \varphi_{Uq}, \varphi_D$ of the levels of $\mathcal A$ base changed to $\mathbb Z/p$ such that $\operatorname{Spec}$ of each, followed by $\iota'_v$, equals $\iota'_v$ followed by $V$, by $F$ followed by $D_\Lambda$, and by $D_\Lambda$ respectively (`hφV`, `hφUq`, `hφD`).
--
--   Under these hypotheses there exist two families of $\mathbb Z/p$-bialgebra maps
--   $$\kappa_0, \kappa_1 : (\mathcal A \otimes \mathbb Z/p).\mathrm{level}\,v \longrightarrow (\mathcal B \otimes \mathbb Z/p).\mathrm{level}\,v \qquad (v \in \mathbb N)$$
--   such that all of the following hold: $\operatorname{Spec}\kappa_{0,v}$ followed by $\iota'_v$ equals $r_0$ at level $v$; each $\kappa_{0,v}$ is injective; $\varepsilon_v \circ \kappa_{0,v} = \kappa_{0,v}$; every $b$ with $\varepsilon_v b = b$ lies in the image of $\kappa_{0,v}$; the transition map of $\mathcal B \otimes \mathbb Z/p$ composed with $\kappa_{0,v+1}$ equals $\kappa_{0,v}$ composed with the transition map of $\mathcal A \otimes \mathbb Z/p$; $\operatorname{Spec}\kappa_{1,v}$ followed by $\iota'_v$ equals $r_1$ at level $v$; each $\kappa_{1,v}$ is injective; $\varepsilon'_v \circ \kappa_{1,v} = \kappa_{1,v}$; every $b$ with $\varepsilon'_v b = b$ lies in the image of $\kappa_{1,v}$; and $\kappa_1$ likewise commutes with the transitions. Moreover the operators are intertwined: $(\varepsilon_v \circ \widetilde{uB}_v) \circ \kappa_{0,v} = \kappa_{0,v} \circ \varphi_{V,v}$; $\widetilde{uB}_v \circ \kappa_{1,v} = \kappa_{1,v} \circ \varphi_{Uq,v}$; and $DB'_v \circ \kappa_{i,v} = \kappa_{i,v} \circ \varphi_{D,v}$ for $i = 0, 1$, where $\widetilde{uB}_v$ denotes the base change of $uB_v$ along $Rh \to \mathbb Z/p$.
--
--   This is the step identifying the two pinned idempotent pieces of the special fibre of the Raynaud quotient $\mathcal B$ with the $p$-divisible group of the level-$(M/p)$ abelian scheme modulo $p$, in a form that carries the $U_p$-, diamond-, Frobenius- and Verschiebung-intertwining relations across the identification. It is used by the two-step tower descent statement [`ModularCurve.exists_twoStepTower_raynaudQuotient_descent_finPts_jHNeronObjectAtP_of_finPtsWitness_of_bridgePins`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_twoStepTower_raynaudQuotient_descent_finPts_jHNeronObjectAtP_of_finPtsWitness_of_bridgePins), the input for the local analysis of the $p$-adic representation attached to $J_H$ at a prime exactly dividing the level.
